<header class=3D"masthead">
  <div class=3D"eyebrow">Flow Integrity Audit</div>
  <h1>Sector Research Dashboard =E2=80=94 end-to-end information flow</h1>
  <p class=3D"dek">A trace of every selection, request, and render in the r=
epository at <code>c:\development\projects\Stocks</code> =E2=80=94 from the=
 sector/market form through the Node request handler and back into the DOM.=
</p>
  <div class=3D"meta-row">
    <span><b>Repository</b> Stocks (4 files, no framework, no DB)</span>
    <span><b>Audited</b> 2026-08-11</span>
    <span><b>Files in scope</b> index.html =C2=B7 script.js =C2=B7 server.m=
js =C2=B7 styles.css</span>
  </div>
</header>

<section id=3D"summary">
  <h2><span class=3D"num">01</span>Executive summary</h2>
  <div class=3D"score-panel">
    <div class=3D"score-ring" style=3D"--pct:58"><span>58</span></div>
    <div>
      <div class=3D"label">Flow integrity score / 100</div>
      <div class=3D"score-summary">Request plumbing (race guards, abort han=
dling, tab state, output escaping) is sound and verified correct. The score=
 is pulled down by a live defect in the recommendation engine itself: the c=
omputed "quality score" and BUY/HOLD signal are fed from a data source that=
 structurally cannot supply fundamentals for two of the app's three market =
options, so the headline output =E2=80=94 not a peripheral field =E2=80=94 =
is silently inconsistent across the one selector that's supposed to scope i=
t.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=3D"stat-row">
    <div class=3D"stat"><div class=3D"n" style=3D"color:var(--crit)">1</div=
><div class=3D"l">Critical finding</div></div>
    <div class=3D"stat"><div class=3D"n" style=3D"color:var(--high)">1</div=
><div class=3D"l">High finding</div></div>
    <div class=3D"stat"><div class=3D"n" style=3D"color:var(--med)">0</div>=
<div class=3D"l">Medium findings</div></div>
    <div class=3D"stat"><div class=3D"n" style=3D"color:var(--low)">3</div>=
<div class=3D"l">Low findings</div></div>
  </div>
  <p><b>Critical continuity failure:</b> the investment score and BUY/ACCUM=
ULATE/HOLD signal are computed from quote fields that are never populated f=
or non-India markets, capping every US/Global stock's score below the BUY t=
hreshold regardless of the company's actual fundamentals. See <a href=3D"ht=
tps://740134fc-3e22-4bd3-bb43-2379af15c102.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1=
786452061-155e/#f1">Finding 1</a>.</p>
  <p><b>High-risk transition failure:</b> selecting India market with certa=
in sector names silently returns US-listed companies while the response sti=
ll labels itself "India," so the market selection is dropped without any si=
gnal to the user. See <a href=3D"https://740134fc-3e22-4bd3-bb43-2379af15c1=
02.frame.claudeusercontent.com/_f/1786452061-155e/#f2">Finding 2</a>.</p>
  <p><b>State integrity assessment:</b> the one piece of genuinely concurre=
nt client state =E2=80=94 "which fetch response is allowed to touch the DOM=
" =E2=80=94 is handled correctly via a monotonic request counter plus <code=
>AbortController</code>. This is the part of the audit template enterprise =
apps most often get wrong, and here it's solid.</p>
</section>

<section id=3D"scope">
  <h2><span class=3D"num">02</span>Audit scope note</h2>
  <div class=3D"scope-note">
    <p style=3D"margin-bottom:8px"><b>This is a 4-file static-HTML + vanill=
a-JS frontend backed by a single Node <code>http</code> server with no data=
base, no auth, no routing library, no state-management library, and no mult=
i-tenancy.</b> The request template this audit follows was written for ente=
rprise applications with tenants, realms, CRUD persistence, workflow engine=
s, and background jobs. None of that architecture exists in this repository=
, so rather than manufacture findings for absent layers, the sections below=
 that don't apply are marked <span class=3D"sev sev-ok">N/A</span> with the=
 reason, and audit effort is concentrated on the flows that actually exist:=
 one form submission, one API endpoint, one render pass, and eight CSS-only=
 tabs.</p>
  </div>
</section>

