<section id=3D"broken">
  <h2><span class=3D"num">04</span>Broken information flows</h2>

  <div class=3D"finding" id=3D"f1">
    <div class=3D"finding-head">
      <h3>Investment score and BUY signal are structurally unreachable for =
non-India markets</h3>
      <span class=3D"sev sev-critical">Critical</span>
    </div>
    <p><code>score()</code> and the per-stock fundamentals block in <code>r=
esearch()</code> read <code>quote.returnOnEquity</code>, <code>quote.profit=
Margins</code>, and <code>quote.debtToEquity</code> off the object returned=
 by <code>getQuote()</code>. That object (built from Yahoo's <code>/v8/fina=
nce/chart/</code> endpoint) only ever sets <code>symbol, shortName, longNam=
e, currency, regularMarketPrice, regularMarketChangePercent, fiftyDayAverag=
e, twoHundredDayAverage, fiftyTwoWeekHigh, fiftyTwoWeekLow, regularMarketVo=
lume</code> =E2=80=94 none of the three fields <code>score()</code> needs. =
The only place those three fields ever get real values is the India-only <c=
ode>indiaFundamentals()</code> scrape, gated by <code>market =3D=3D=3D 'Ind=
ia'</code> at line 165.</p>
    <p>Net effect: for <code>market=3D"United States"</code> or <code>marke=
t=3D"Global"</code> =E2=80=94 two of the three dropdown options =E2=80=94 e=
very stock's score is built from only the two moving-average bonuses (max 5=
0 + 8 + 6 =3D <b>64</b>), while India-market stocks can reach the full 50=
=E2=80=9395 range. <code>signal()</code> in <span class=3D"filepath">script=
.js:9</span> requires =E2=89=A570 for "BUY," which is mathematically unreac=
hable outside the India market. The sector-level <code>recommendation</code=
> ("OVERWEIGHT" at avgScore =E2=89=A5 70) inherits the same ceiling.</p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Files</dt><dd><span class=3D"filepath">server.mjs:27-34</span> (s=
core), <span class=3D"filepath">server.mjs:124-142</span> (getQuote), <span=
 class=3D"filepath">server.mjs:165-168</span> (fundamentals gate), <span cl=
ass=3D"filepath">script.js:9</span> (signal thresholds)</dd>
      <dt>Repro</dt><dd>Select market <code>United States</code>, sector <c=
ode>Semiconductor</code>, submit. Every row in Top stocks tops out around 5=
0-64 and never shows a BUY tag. Repeat with market <code>India</code>, sect=
or <code>Power</code> =E2=80=94 BUY tags appear at score =E2=89=A5 70 for o=
therwise comparable companies.</dd>
      <dt>Root cause</dt><dd>An incomplete migration: the code comment at <=
span class=3D"filepath">server.mjs:121-123</span> explains the switch away =
from Yahoo's old multi-symbol quote endpoint (401 without a browser crumb) =
to the chart endpoint =E2=80=94 but the downstream field references to the =
old endpoint's fundamentals were never updated or removed.</dd>
      <dt>Why it matters</dt><dd>This isn't a missing display field =E2=80=
=94 it's the app's headline output (score, BUY/HOLD tag, sector recommendat=
ion) silently meaning something different depending on which market radio t=
he user picked, with no disclosure anywhere in the UI.</dd>
      <dt>Fix</dt><dd>Either source comparable fundamentals for US/Global e=
quities so the score basis matches India, or make the scoring formula adapt=
ive =E2=80=94 redistribute the India-only 28 points across the technical-on=
ly signals when <code>market !=3D=3D 'India'</code> =E2=80=94 or relabel th=
e non-India score as "technical score" so the 0=E2=80=93100 scale stops imp=
lying parity it can't deliver.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <div class=3D"finding" id=3D"f2">
    <div class=3D"finding-head">
      <h3>Sector preset silently overrides the market selection for four se=
ctors</h3>
      <span class=3D"sev sev-high">High</span>
    </div>
    <p><code>tickerList()</code> (<span class=3D"filepath">server.mjs:91-11=
9</span>) only special-cases the market argument for presets whose <b>first=
</b> symbol carries a <code>.NS</code>/<code>.BO</code> suffix (the sugar/b=
iofuel family) =E2=80=94 that check at line 102 skips the India preset when=
 a non-India market is selected, as the surrounding comment documents. The =
<code>semiconductor</code>, <code>fintech</code>, <code>renewable energy</c=
ode>, and <code>banking</code> presets in the same dictionary have no such =
guard: they are returned unconditionally regardless of the <code>market</co=
de> argument.</p>
    <p>Because <code>research()</code> echoes the requested <code>market</c=
ode> straight into the response (<span class=3D"filepath">server.mjs:175</s=
pan>, <code>market</code> field) rather than deriving it from the actual re=
sult set, the dashboard title and status badge will read e.g. "Semiconducto=
r Research Dashboard =E2=80=93 India" while the table underneath lists NVDA=
, AVGO, AMD, TSM =E2=80=94 US-listed companies with no NSE/BSE presence.</p=
>
    <dl>
      <dt>Files</dt><dd><span class=3D"filepath">server.mjs:8-15</span> (pr=
esets dict), <span class=3D"filepath">server.mjs:91-102</span> (selection l=
ogic), <span class=3D"filepath">server.mjs:175</span> (market echoed verbat=
im)</dd>
      <dt>Repro</dt><dd>Market <code>India</code>, sector <code>Fintech</co=
de> (or Semiconductor / Renewable energy / Banking), submit. Title says Ind=
ia; symbols are NU, PYPL, SQ, SOFI=E2=80=A6</dd>
      <dt>Downstream consequence</dt><dd><code>indiaFundamentals()</code> s=
till fires (market passed is literally <code>'India'</code>) and scrapes <c=
ode>screener.in/company/NVDA/=E2=80=A6</code> for a US ticker =E2=80=94 cau=
ght by the try/catch, returns <code>{source:'Unavailable'}</code>, but it's=
 a wasted round trip built on a mismatched premise. <code>newsFor()</code> =
also scopes the Google News query to <code>gl=3DIN</code> for companies tha=
t trade nowhere near India.</dd>
      <dt>Fix</dt><dd>Give these four sectors the same three-way (India/US/=
Global) preset structure already used for <code>power</code>/<code>utility<=
/code>/<code>energy</code> at <span class=3D"filepath">server.mjs:66-80</sp=
an>, or drop the unconditional presets in favor of the Yahoo-search-plus-<c=
ode>isInSelectedMarket</code>-filter path that already exists for unlisted =
sectors.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <div class=3D"finding">
    <div class=3D"finding-head">
      <h3>Five computed fields are sent by the API and rendered nowhere</h3=
>
      <span class=3D"sev sev-low">Low</span>
    </div>
    <p><code>forwardPE</code>, <code>eps</code>, <code>roa</code>, <code>ma=
rgin</code>, and <code>revenueGrowth</code> (plus the aggregate <code>avera=
ges.margin</code> / <code>averages.growth</code>) are computed in <code>res=
earch()</code> from the same missing <code>q.*</code> fields as Finding 1, =
so they are always <code>null</code> for every stock in every market =E2=80=
=94 and none of the four render templates in <span class=3D"filepath">scrip=
t.js</span> (leaders, metrics, valuation, technical tables) reference any o=
f them. They're dead weight end to end: computed, serialized, transmitted, =
and discarded.</p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Files</dt><dd><span class=3D"filepath">server.mjs:167-175</span>,=
 no consumer in <span class=3D"filepath">script.js</span></dd>
      <dt>Fix</dt><dd>Either wire real sources for these fields and add tab=
le columns, or delete the dead references =E2=80=94 low priority, no user-v=
isible defect today, but it's the clearest evidence the endpoint migration =
in Finding 1 was left half-done.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

